Measured on a detached copy of this board (a temp repo with no remote, fed
`refs/karr/*` by path) while implementing #138.

`karr metrics` excludes a card from cycle time when its `started` precedes its
own `created`:

    my $start_measurable =
      defined $started && ( !defined $created || $started >= $created );

After `karr repair --yes` clamps `started` to `created` (#138) that condition is
true by construction for every card it touched, so the guard excludes nothing
any more -- and `unusable_timestamps`, the figure whose whole job is to say how
many cards are missing from the averages, drops to zero.

    before repair:  lead 6h38m over 134 | cycle 4h44m over 42  | efficiency 17.6% | unusable 75
    after  repair:  lead 6h38m over 134 | cycle 0h16m over 117 | efficiency  3.4% | unusable 0

The average cycle time of 16 minutes is not a measurement. 42 of those 117
cycle samples are negative, because the same old karr also wrote `completed` as
a bare date and it falls before the clamped `started` (companion ticket on the
`completed` stamp). The command now reports a figure built on them with no
caveat at all -- the confidently wrong number its own POD says it exists not to
print.

The relevant POD paragraph ("Neither average is clamped: a card whose
`completed` precedes its `created` ... contributes a negative duration and is
left visible as one") was written for a hand-edited outlier. It is not wrong,
but it does not cover 36% of the sample being impossible.

What is probably wanted: a second ordering check -- a cycle time may not be
negative, i.e. `completed >= started` -- counted into `unusable_timestamps` the
same way the start check is, so the note keeps naming what was left out. That
is a metrics-semantics decision (karr-board-worker), which is why #138
deliberately did not touch `lib/App/karr/Cmd/Metrics.pm`.
Fixed in d21f0b8: second ordering guard (completed >= started) on the cycle average, counted into unusable_timestamps. POD/skill docs and Changes updated, regression test t/141-metrics-cycle-order.t. Lead time deliberately untouched -- see #139.
